<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Sicyon</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Sicyon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">City of the northern Peloponnese ruled by Greece's longest tyranny, a famed centre of painting and sculpture and the home of Aratus, leader of the Achaean League.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sicyon lay in a fertile plain west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. It was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and became an independent city. In the 7th century BCE it fell under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthagorids">Orthagorid</a> tyrants, whose rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called the longest-lasting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes-of-sicyon">Cleisthenes</a>, early 6th century BCE, renamed the Dorian tribes, fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> and helped win the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sacred-war">First Sacred War</a>; his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agariste-of-sicyon">Agariste</a> married into the Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonids</a>. Sicyon was later an oligarchy and a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>. It became famous for bronze sculpture, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a>, and for its school of painting, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apelles">Apelles</a> studied.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> moved the city to a defensible plateau in 303 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> freed Sicyon from tyranny in 251 BCE and joined it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a>, which he then led. Sicyon passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE, gaining from the destruction of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sicyon.jpg" alt="Sicyon" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sikyon,_Greece_(23187324446).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sicyon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Orthagoras, Cleisthenes, Aratus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sikyon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=sicyon" aria-label="Sicyon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5246 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sicyon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5566 1068L5562 1098M5566 1068L5549 1060M5566 1068L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5562 1098h0M5549 1060h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5566 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5566 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=sicyon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

